Warmed draw attribution (2026-11-04)
  Persistent byte reuse alone did not reach the routine five-minute target. Two existing
  CachedPatchRequiresRepresentedCoverage cases were measured with temporary stage clocks;
  both retained all original calls/assertions and passed. Their combined draw-wrapper interval
  was 5.799s, versus .499s for program/camera preparation and .033s for reads/assertions.
  A separate refined diagnostic added one same-program draw after all original assertions;
  the repeated decision output matched exactly. First helper calls took 2.527s / 2.914s,
  while repeats took 2.521ms / 1.838ms. The largest first-call region, 2.395s / 2.912s, spans
  pass completion and boundary return. These are CPU wall scopes, not exclusive GPU timings;
  outer helper totals include nested diagnostic record-writing overhead.
  Both temporary source changes are restored exactly. Fresh Release compilation reproduced
  the pre-probe test DLL, with probe markers absent, unchanged 909 shader modules and intact
  434-entry cache. Only the permanent persistent-cache/documentation changes remain.
  The proposed next pilot would share one live diagnostic executable within the 17-case numerical
  budget class, explicitly reset every input and retain fresh per-case scenes/cameras. This changes
  current per-case executable ownership; the user approved this bounded pilot on 2026-11-04.
  Cold/lifecycle tests are outside it. No wider live-program cache is included. A fresh-process
  17-case pre-change control passed 17/17 in 46.999s with retained executable bytes; the candidate
  will be compared against that exact selection, not against unrelated whole-suite timings.
  The candidate retains all 17 original rows, draws, motion samples, references and tolerances;
  one separate GPU control exercises full 1,088-byte reset, captured-input immutability, empty
  borrowed references, exclusive/expired leases and final owner/platform retirement. Class-owned
  dependencies are initialized before per-case platform scopes, and per-case reductions remain
  unchanged. Source review caught and corrected a disposal guard that committed retirement before
  rejecting an active lease; the control now rejects that disposal and verifies later cleanup.
  Corrected-source builds pass in 13.921s Debug / 13.626s Release. The original 17 cases pass
  in 10.081s / 8.481s with identities and numerical output unchanged from the Release control.
  The separate 18-case selection passes in 24.409s / 24.639s: one new fixture control, seven
  shallow-water cases, the positive-view-Z case and nine explicit cold shader mechanics checks.
  These are 35 distinct scoped cases per configuration, zero failures/skips. Discovery is 5,947
  per configuration: one added control and no removed cases. Both test DLLs and all five pilot
  sources remained stable during verification; all 909 shader modules per configuration match
  baseline. The Release executable cache retains the same 434 entries and 17,523,863 bytes.
  Independent source, corrected-lifecycle and raw-result reviews are clean. Intermediate builds
  and namespace-failure receipts are retained separately and do not supply passing-test evidence.
  The recorded Release pair demonstrates the local pilot result, not a stable percentage or
  whole-suite timing. The latest complete warmed Release run (641.200s) predates this pilot;
  no current full Debug/Release runtime or five-minute success is established. The user subsequently
  approved class-scoped reuse in numerical-only GPU suites on 2026-11-04, starting with shallow-water,
  UV and pixel-normal checks. Native, reload, allocation and disposal subjects retain fresh owners.
  Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/WaterBudgetClassFixturePilot/20261104T150039Z/.
  Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/WarmWaterAttribution/20261104T142714Z/
  and ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/DrawAttribution/20261104T143728Z/.

Numerical class-fixture rollout (2026-11-04; initial three-suite batch qualified)
  Preserve every original case, numerical reference, assertion, tolerance and sample. Retain fresh
  case-owned scene textures and cameras, with complete packed-input and borrowed-reference resets
  for each exclusive lease. Owners remain local to one test class; there is no global live-program
  cache, GPU concurrency or process split. Class fixtures must initialize before per-case platform
  scopes and retire before the collection context. Cold and lifecycle subjects remain isolated.
  The matched pre-change Release selections pass 56/56 with retained caches: shallow continuity
  7 cases in 26.044s, UV 37 cases in 24.198s, pixel-normal 12 cases in 12.576s. These are separate
  process walls, including startup and teardown. Sources, test DLL, 909 shader modules and the
  434-entry executable cache remained unchanged.
  Corrected-source builds pass in 19.265s Debug / 58.757s Release. An earlier Debug build failed
  on a missing generic closing bracket in the shallow fixture factory; it ran no tests and its
  separate receipts are retained. The original 56 cases pass in each configuration with zero
  name or numerical-output differences. Candidate per-class walls are 13.486s / 11.831s for
  shallow continuity, 9.024s / 7.485s for UV, and 8.621s / 6.618s for pixel-normal (Debug / Release).
  Supporting selections pass 30/30 in 13.072s / 9.942s: the original 17 budget cases, the existing
  budget isolation control, three new separate fixture controls and nine explicit cold mechanics
  checks. These are 86 distinct focused cases per configuration, zero failures/skips. Discovery
  is 5,950 per configuration: exactly three new controls and no removals from the pilot candidate.
  All 14 rollout source hashes and both test DLLs remain stable; 909 shader modules per configuration
  match baseline. Release retains the same 434 valid cache entries and 17,523,863 bytes; Debug has
  five valid entries and 708,591 bytes. Source and scoped raw-result reviews are clean.
  One complete warmed Release AllTests run, with the retained cache and no intervening build,
  passes 5,949 with zero failures and seven retained skips in 574.267s (9m34.3s). All 5,952 prior
  full-run identities remain; four fixture controls are added (the budget pilot plus this batch's
  three). There are 5,956 unique runtime rows and 5,950 adapter identities; unchanged dynamic Poisson
  expansion accounts for the six-row difference. The nine explicit cold checks, real-GC stress
  and all four fixture controls pass. Skip identities and reasons exactly match the prior warm run.
  The 14 rollout sources, test DLL and 909 Release shader modules remain unchanged through this
  full run. The cache remains valid at 434 entries and 17,523,863 bytes. The command excludes builds
  and includes stress. No fresh full Debug run is claimed. The earlier warmed 641.200s sample
  predates both the budget pilot and this rollout; the shorter current sample does not isolate
  either change or establish a stable percentage. The routine five-minute target remains unmet.
  Remaining numerical cost leaders in this run include WaterRefractionTests (33 rows / 19.236s),
  AmbientOcclusionCompositeTests (6 / 12.848s), LumOnDirectWorldProbeVisibilityTests (62 / 11.420s)
  and AmbientOcclusionHorizonBehaviorTests (10 / 9.896s). These are summed case durations, not
  predicted wall savings or proof of safe sharing. Larger mixed runtime/publication/ownership
  subjects require method-level classification before reuse; their lifecycle guarantees remain.
  Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/NumericalClassFixtureRollout/20261104T154339Z/.

Runtime-boundary audit and further numerical reuse (2026-11-04; AO batch qualified)
  The user authorized the next audit and numerical reuse batch. SurfaceLightingConsumerRuntimeTests
  and SurfaceLightingSpatialRuntimeTests retain per-case runtime/program owners. The consumer
  methods exercise worker retries, upload admission, publication, obsolete completions, recreation
  and callback disposal. Spatial methods exercise temporal anchors/history, physical ring retention,
  signed streaming generations, dark-room publication and mixed-consumer replacement/invalidation.
  SurfaceLightingConsumerRuntimeFixture creates its API-owned production shader library before
  registered renderers demand programs; RuntimeLightingPrograms.Initialize asserts initially empty
  demand state. Teardown stops consumers before disposing the library and its cache/platform
  dependencies. No independent numerical reset/lease boundary was found: sharing these live owners
  would cross API, callback, configuration and publication lifetimes. Existing validated executable
  byte caching remains available. The audit recommends no changes to these two runtime families
  under the numerical-only reuse authorization; a broader lifetime redesign is outside this batch.
  Evidence: GPU/SurfaceLightingConsumerRuntimeTests.cs (registered lifetime, worker, query and
  disposal cases), GPU/SurfaceLightingSpatialRuntimeTests.cs (camera/ring/streaming/mixed cases),
  GPU/Fixtures/SurfaceLightingConsumerRuntimeFixture.cs and GPU/Fixtures/RuntimeLightingPrograms.cs,
  all paths relative to ../VanillaGraphicsExpanded.Tests/.
  The fresh pre-change Release baseline passes 120/120 with no failures/skips in separate retained-
  cache processes: consumer runtime 13 / 35.289s; spatial runtime 6 / 19.955s; water refraction
  33 / 29.600s; AO composite 6 / 12.286s; direct world-probe visibility 62 / 15.412s. Sources, DLL,
  all 909 shader modules and the valid 434-entry cache remain unchanged.
  AO composite is the narrow implementation: each row repeats VerifyComposition with
  the same compile options. Reuse one program within the existing case-owned Programs lifetime,
  passing it to each helper call and clearing borrowed sampler/camera references before scenario
  resources retire. All six rows, numerical sweeps, draw order, references and tolerances remain.
  No class-scoped production owner or new production reset API is needed. Source-derived program
  creations fall from 96 to six: three single-scenario rows plus three rows of 31 scenarios.
  All 96 VerifyComposition calls and their 384 draws remain. These are source-derived counts,
  not instrumented compilation or driver-work savings. Independent source review is clean.
  Canonical Debug/Release builds pass in 108.887s / 100.694s. Dedicated AO selections pass 6/6
  in 9.985s / 6.491s, with unchanged case identities and output; the matched Release baseline is
  12.286s. Separate regression processes pass 166/166 in 99.562s / 75.882s: the other 114 baseline
  cases plus 52 related AO checks. These are 172 distinct passing checks per configuration, zero
  failures/skips. Both regression selections retain two spatial MixedConsumers diagnostic-output
  differences from the standalone Release controls, confined to printed minRGB values. Yaw samples,
  threshold/replacement decisions, ray counts and asserted outcomes remain unchanged. The changed
  AO methods do not execute in those regression processes. Raw differences remain in the receipts;
  no exact regression-stdout equivalence is claimed across these differently grouped runs.
  Discovery remains 5,950 per configuration, with no cases added or removed. The sole changed test
  source and both test DLLs remain stable; all 909 shader modules per configuration match baseline.
  The Release cache remains valid at 434 entries and 17,523,863 bytes. The dedicated timing pair
  supports the local result, not a stable percentage or whole-suite speedup. No complete suite is
  rerun for this test-only batch: the last complete warmed Release measurement, 574.267s, predates
  it. No fresh full Debug/Release or under-five-minute result is established by these scoped checks.
  WaterRefractionTests executes its comparison once per ordinary case. Cross-case sharing would
  require persistent API/asset composition, immutable compile variants (quality, resolution and
  capture paths), and reset of three input buffers, 12 samplers and camera/lighting/fog/material
  state; diagnostic methods also use explicit per-case asset overrides. Leave this family unchanged.
  DirectWorldProbeVisibilityTestBase already caches each compiled variant within its test instance.
  Cross-case reuse would require a class-owned identity/define dictionary and complete resets for
  debug, atlas-gather and SH9-gather bindings, near-field staging resources, frame/world publications
  and case-owned LightingResources. No narrow existing reset seam was found; leave it unchanged.
  These are limits of the current narrow reuse design, not proof that broader optimization is
  impossible. Preserve their fresh owners rather than changing production lifecycle contracts.
  Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/NextNumericalReuseBatch/20261104T165742Z/.

Post-draw native-binary export probe (2026-11-04; measured and removed)
  The user approved a temporary isolated experiment: export the heavy water executable before
  and after its first draw, reload each payload into fresh owners, and compare first/repeated
  draw costs and output. Production cache policy and existing tests are unchanged. The temporary
  Diagnostic Fact mirrors CachedPatchRequiresRepresentedCoverage(false), including its original
  four result assertions, 128-square inputs, budget two and one-pixel seven-attachment output.
  Each process uses a unique in-memory ProgramBinaryStore. ABBA and BAAB denote before/after
  reload orders, with four fresh cached owners per process and fresh scene resources per trial.
  Corrected-source Release build passes in 18.902s. Both diagnostic processes pass exactly one
  case with no skips in 28.943s / 29.940s. All eight reloads assert actual native binary hits;
  both source preparations assert misses. Seed/repeated/reloaded observations match across all
  seven attachments: 504 bitwise float comparisons across the two processes, plus the original
  independent expected-result checks. Before/after payload hashes differ in both processes,
  but every payload has native format 36385 and length 413,252 bytes. Opaque byte differences
  alone do not identify an optimization or explain timing.
  Pre-draw payload reloads take 3.533–4.103s for the first RenderQuadTo helper call; post-draw
  payloads take 3.209–3.822s. Loading plus first draw and explicit completion totals
  3.551–4.121s / 3.226–3.842s, respectively. Reload preparation itself takes 14.732–19.555ms;
  repeated draw plus completion takes .927–1.829ms. Fresh source creation/loading takes
  8.264s / 8.999s including automatic initial cache export; first source draws take .171s / .164s.
  The additional explicit pre-draw exports take 1.435ms / 1.925ms; post-draw exports take
  1.116ms / 1.163ms. Successful cached reloads do not re-export under the existing policy.
  These are CPU wall scopes: RenderQuadTo includes preparation, submission and boundary return,
  not exclusive GPU execution. Driver warmth is shared within each process and uncontrolled
  across processes. Fresh native owners remain retained by the case's Programs owner until
  teardown. Counterbalancing does not make these eight observations a stable performance study.
  Post-draw export does not eliminate the multi-second first-use cost in this workload; overlapping
  ranges do not establish a stable benefit or justify a production cache-policy change. The
  routine five-minute objective remains unmet. No whole-suite timing or new Debug pass is claimed.
  All 434 default persistent cache payloads, their modification times and index recency remain
  unchanged through measurement (17,523,863 bytes, zero invalid entries). The experiment writes
  only its isolated in-memory store. All 909 Release shader modules match baseline.
  Review corrected missing independent seed oracles and partial-construction cleanup before any
  measurement. The superseded 148.481s build supplied no results; a zero-selection launch inherited
  ordinary runner exclusions and was rejected by the row-count guard. Actual measurements use
  receipt-local diagnostic-inclusive settings. Source-hash guards rejected a comment edit after
  premature freeze; final measured source hash is recorded with its archived bytes in the receipts.
  The temporary source is removed and its exact final bytes archived outside the compile tree.
  Restored Release compilation passes in 17.035s with the Csc task confirmed; probe class and
  environment markers are absent. Discovery returns to the original 5,950 identities with zero
  differences. Nine cold mechanics cases and both existing cached-patch controls pass 11/11 in
  10.075s. Captured original source inputs, all 909 Release shader modules and the Debug test DLL
  remain unchanged. The fresh Release DLL differs from the pre-probe hash; its generated metadata
  identifies the current commit, but the old DLL bytes were not archived, so the exact cause of
  that difference is unproven. Restoration is established by original sources, fresh compilation,
  marker absence, exact discovery and passing controls, not byte-identical Release output.
  Control execution advances one default-cache read-recency record without adding or rewriting
  binaries; all 434 payload hashes and modification times remain unchanged. Only this evidence
  record remains modified. No production or permanent test change results from the experiment.
  Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/PostDrawBinaryProbe/20261104T184746Z/.

First-use boundary wait attribution (2026-11-04; measured and removed)
  The user authorized continued diagnosis of the persistent first-draw stall. Temporary counters
  covered RenderQuadTo, boundary.Run, EndRenderPass, cleanup by declared resource order, drawing-
  state restoration, cache boundary release and ReadBoundaryError. No native interception generator
  changed. Fixed thread-local counters performed no allocation or I/O during collection; all output
  formatting, explicit waits and readback remained outside the baseline capture. Timed regions are
  inclusive: error polls include entry queries plus polls nested within cleanup and restoration.
  Cache boundary release is narrower than the outer RHI logical release. This is managed CPU wall
  around selected calls, not complete native-call coverage or exclusive GPU execution time.
  The diagnostic used an actual default-store cache hit and the exact non-irregular cached-patch
  input, four independent original assertions and bitwise equality of all seven attachment tuples
  between first and repeated draws. A corrected-source Release build passed in 49.700s after one
  missing-System-import build failure (30.402s, no tests); the actual diagnostic passed 1/1 in
  9.646s with no skips. The first helper took 2868.218ms, including boundary.Run 2780.315ms,
  binding cleanup 2716.259ms, EndRenderPass .201ms, drawing-state restore 5.204ms and cache-boundary
  release .140ms. Error polls totalled 2713.518ms across 165 calls, with one 2712.491ms maximum.
  The repeat helper took 2.747ms: binding cleanup .172ms, 30 polls totalling .194ms with .025ms
  maximum. The overlapping cleanup/poll intervals localize where the observed wait surfaces; they
  are not separate additive costs or proof of driver/JIT/GPU causality. Program creation/loading
  was not separately timed by this experiment.
  One bounded follow-up added an explicitly timed existing WaitIdle after DrawIndexed and before
  EndRenderPass through the existing afterDraw callback. Its opt-in environment flag left the
  default path unchanged; callback allocation preceded capture. All original calls, guards, error
  checks and output assertions remained. The RHI/backend path permits this and calls GL.Finish
  between context checks. The control build passed in 18.137s; its fresh-process diagnostic passed
  1/1 in 9.702s. First helper time was 3032.162ms, of which the inserted completion wait took
  2870.969ms. Binding cleanup fell to 3.044ms and all error polls to 1.002ms, maximum .101ms.
  Poll counts remained 165 first / 30 repeat, and the repeat helper took 2.977ms. Original oracles
  and all 28 first/repeat float bit comparisons passed in each process.
  The observed latency moves from checked polling to an earlier explicit completion wait while
  the complete helper remains approximately three seconds. This supports waiting for outstanding
  work rather than a demonstrated error-bookkeeping cost. It does not establish the underlying
  GPU/driver/JIT mechanism or a stable timing percentage. Removing error checks is not supported
  as a runtime fix by this evidence. Production validation and cache policy remain unchanged.
  Both probes retain 909 identical Release shader modules and the same 434 valid cache payloads
  (17,523,863 bytes), with unchanged binary hashes/modification times and ordinary read-recency
  updates reported separately. Raw sources, binaries, commands and timings are archived.
  The three original source files are now restored byte-for-byte and both temporary files removed.
  Restored Release compilation passes in 37.481s with actual main and test Csc tasks confirmed.
  Release main/test DLLs, Debug main/test DLLs and the generator DLL are byte-identical to their
  archived pre-probe copies. Temporary markers are absent; discovery returns to the original
  5,950 identities with zero differences. Nine cold mechanics cases and both cached-patch controls
  pass 11/11 in 10.965s. Release shader modules and manifest remain unchanged. The same 434 cache
  payload hashes/modification times remain valid; ordinary read-recency updates are separated from
  binary writes in each receipt. Independent source, raw measurement and control reviews are clean.
  Only this evidence record remains modified. No complete-suite timing, new Debug qualification,
  production optimization or five-minute success is claimed.
  Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/FirstDrawNativeCallProbe/20261104T195313Z/.

First-use process CPU and GPU timestamp probe (2026-11-04; measured and removed)
  The user authorized further diagnosis of the first-use delay. A temporary test and an appended
  optional beforeDraw callback bracketed DrawIndexed with timestamp commands; the existing
  afterDraw callback issued the end timestamp. Four query objects, two equivalent one-pixel
  seven-attachment outputs and all scene resources were created before timing. Separate outputs
  preserved first/repeat images without readback or query-result synchronization between draws.
  The executable had a confirmed default-store cache hit. The exact non-irregular cached-patch
  values, four original independent oracles and 28 first/repeat float bit comparisons were retained.
  CPU endpoints used refreshed aggregate process TotalProcessorTime around each helper call;
  the observation includes all process threads and sampling overhead, not just the render thread.
  The probe required a 64-bit timestamp counter, ordered nonnegative raw timestamps and checked
  subtraction. It explicitly reported unavailable caching/counter support rather than accepting
  missing data. Result availability was bounded after both draws and WaitIdle; retrieval/readback
  occurred outside the helper clocks. GPU timestamps measure ordered command-completion intervals,
  not exclusive GPU-busy or shader execution time; submission gaps and work outside the bracket
  limit causal interpretation. See [Khronos ARB_timer_query](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/KhronosGroup/OpenGL-Registry/main/extensions/ARB/ARB_timer_query.txt),
  especially timer semantics and issues 1, 5 and 8.
  Corrected-source Release compilation passes in 16.778s. The actual diagnostic passes 1/1 with
  no skips in 10.178s. First RenderQuadTo wall is 3344.548ms, aggregate process CPU is 6375 core-ms,
  and the ordered GPU timestamp interval is 17.830912ms. Repeat helper wall is 3.6246ms and its
  timestamp interval .007168ms. Repeat process CPU is reported as zero at this sampling resolution;
  this does not mean zero CPU work. The first CPU total can exceed wall time because threads run
  concurrently. The final completion wait is .618ms, availability polling 1.5275ms, result retrieval
  .134ms and combined seven-MRT readback 32.2544ms, all outside both helper measurement windows.
  All original assertions and bit comparisons pass. Raw 64-bit timestamp endpoints are preserved
  as decimal strings in the receipt to avoid precision loss during JSON postprocessing.
  This shows substantial process CPU activity during first use alongside a much shorter timed
  DrawIndexed command interval. It does not identify the CPU component, establish driver/JIT
  causality, exclude GPU work outside the timestamp window, or demonstrate a speedup. One
  first/repeat pair is not a stable performance study. No production or cache policy change follows.
  An earlier 15.090s build failed on an out-of-scope expected-color value and supplied no results;
  review also corrected narrow-counter wrap ambiguity before measurement. Failed and superseded
  source/build receipts remain separate from the corrected measured source.
  All 909 Release shader modules and the same 434 valid default-cache payloads remain unchanged
  (17,523,863 bytes); ordinary read-recency updates are recorded separately. The temporary test
  is removed and the helper restored byte-for-byte. Restored Release compilation passes in
  18.246s with actual test Csc confirmed. All five captured DLLs (Release main/test, Debug main/test
  and generator) match their pre-probe bytes; temporary class and added callback markers are absent.
  Discovery returns to the original 5,950 identities with zero differences. Nine cold mechanics
  cases and both cached-patch controls pass 11/11 in 10.800s. Shader modules/manifest and all 434
  cache payload hashes/modification times remain unchanged; control reads advance one recency
  record. Only this evidence record remains modified. No new full-suite runtime, Debug qualification
  or five-minute result is established.
  Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/FirstDrawCpuGpuProbe/20261104T203742Z/.

First-use hot-thread CPU and entry provenance (2026-11-04; measured and removed)
  The user requested continued diagnosis of the first-use CPU activity. One temporary Diagnostic
  test measured the same cached heavy water program and original scene/oracles, with two separate
  first/repeat MRT targets and bitwise equality across all seven attachments. Process helper CPU
  and wall clocks remained separate from non-atomic own-process thread censuses. Thread identities
  used OS ID plus creation time; new, exited, reused, unreadable and unknown samples were reported
  separately. Module enumeration and thread scans stayed outside the helper clocks. The repeat
  follows a thread census and is not an undisturbed immediate repeat. No systemwide tracing,
  thread suspension/injection, privilege changes or other-process inspection occurred.
  Baseline Release build passes in 24.029s; its diagnostic passes 1/1 with a cache hit in 13.530s.
  First helper wall is 2050.011ms, process CPU 4140.625 core-ms (user 2671.875, kernel 1468.750).
  The render thread accounts for 2000ms; two workers account for 1906.25ms and 250ms. All 21 thread
  identities match, with no new/exited/reused/unreadable identities. Observed thread CPU totals
  4156.25ms, leaving a -15.625ms process-minus-thread residual from scan/endpoint skew. The .NET
  StartAddress property yields no usable address, so that run establishes no module attribution.
  One opt-in metadata follow-up dynamically resolves the system ntdll NtQueryInformationThread
  export. Query-only thread handles must belong to this process and match the sample's creation
  FILETIME; success requires a valid status, pointer-sized returned data and nonzero address.
  Handles/library references are released, and unsupported/error cases remain unknown. The .NET
  address and native fallback fields remain distinct. Microsoft documents this start-address query
  and ABI but the installed SDK omits its numeric value; ordinal 9 is corroborated by the primary
  PHNT header and explicitly treated as a guarded current-Windows diagnostic convention, not a
  portable production dependency. Sources: [Microsoft NtQueryInformationThread](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winternl/nf-winternl-ntqueryinformationthread)
  and [PHNT thread information classes](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/winsiderss/phnt/master/ntpsapi.h).
  The metadata control builds in 14.682s and passes 1/1 with a cache hit in 7.429s. First helper
  wall is 2269.343ms and process CPU 4484.375 core-ms (user 2906.250, kernel 1578.125). The render
  thread accounts for 2187.5ms (671.875 user, 1515.625 kernel); its entry maps to coreclr.dll.
  A worker accounts for 2078.125ms (2015.625 user, 62.5 kernel), with a successfully queried
  Win32 start address mapping to nvoglv64.dll in both before/after module censuses. The installed
  file identifies NVIDIA Corporation, NVIDIA Compatible OpenGL ICD, version 32.0.16.1088.
  Another coreclr-entry worker accounts for 234.375ms. All 21 identities match with no losses;
  matched CPU totals 4500ms and the residual is -15.625ms. Thread scans take 19.343ms before and
  16.6796ms after the first draw, outside its helper clock; their CPU/skew costs remain explicit.
  Repeat-after-census helper wall is 1.9888ms; process CPU reports zero at this resolution, while
  the longer thread scan window observes 15.625ms. Neither observation implies zero CPU work.
  Both runs retain the original four expected-result assertions and all 28 float bit comparisons.
  The supported finding is substantial CPU activity on the render thread and a worker with
  NVIDIA-driver entry provenance. Start-address provenance is not a current instruction pointer
  or sampled execution stack. In particular, a coreclr-entry thread can call native code; its
  kernel CPU cannot be assigned to coreclr execution. No exact driver/compiler function, shader
  compilation diagnosis, stable percentage or production optimization is established.
  Both probes preserve 909 Release shader modules and the same 434 cache payload hashes and
  modification times (17,523,863 bytes), with ordinary read-recency updates separated. The
  temporary test is removed. Restored Release compilation passes in 12.528s with actual test Csc
  confirmed. All five saved DLLs (Release main/test, Debug main/test and generator) match their
  pre-probe bytes; temporary class, environment and native-interop markers are absent. Discovery
  returns to the original 5,950 identities with zero differences. Nine cold mechanics cases and
  both cached-patch controls pass 11/11 in 7.790s. Shader modules/manifest and all 434 cache payload
  hashes/modification times remain unchanged; control reads update one recency record. Only this
  evidence record remains modified. No new full-suite time, Debug qualification or under-five-
  minute result is claimed.
  Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/FirstDrawThreadCpuProbe/20261104T232516Z/.

First-use native CPU-stack capture (2026-11-05; measured and removed)
  A temporary Diagnostic test retained the same cached water executable, original scene and four
  independent oracles, with separate first/repeat targets and all 28 float bit comparisons.
  Both draws ran consecutively without intervening logging, waits or readback. Process identity,
  render thread ID and invariant-string QPC/UTC endpoints identify the measured helper windows.
  After explicit Windows administrator approval, only the owned recorder ran elevated; the test
  retained its ordinary token. A bounded memory-mode WPR session sampled CPU stacks and required
  process/thread/image metadata. Recording was briefly systemwide; analysis selects only the test
  process and marked windows. The owned session stopped successfully; existing loggers were left
  untouched. All 38 original logger names remain; one additional unattributed GUID logger appears
  afterward, so identical global logger inventories are not claimed. No command targeted it.
  The 75,497,472-byte trace covers both windows and reports zero lost events/buffers.
  The diagnostic passes 1/1 without skips on an actual cache hit. First helper wall is 3192.048ms;
  immediate repeat is 2.950ms. Original assertions and bit comparisons pass. First-window exports
  contain 6,351 sampled CPU hits across the test process. Exclusive leaf-module hits include
  nvgpucomp64.dll 2,817 (44.36%), ntkrnlmp.exe 2,254 (35.49%), ntdll.dll 581 (9.15%), clrjit.dll
  302 (4.76%) and nvoglv64.dll 129 (2.03%); unknown-module hits total 19 (.30%). These are shares
  of CPU samples across concurrent threads, not shares of elapsed draw time. Inclusive stack
  counts remain separate and must not be summed as exclusive CPU costs. The repeat has only four
  samples and cannot support a meaningful module distribution. Local symbols only were used;
  unresolved private functions do not establish a specific compiler operation or kernel cause.
  The render thread has 3,105 samples, including 2,172 kernel, 573 ntdll and 114 nvoglv leaf hits.
  One NVIDIA-entry worker has 2,920 samples, including 2,817 nvgpucomp64 leaf hits (96.47%). These
  two threads account for 6,025 of 6,351 hits; the remaining 326 process hits were not expanded
  further. Five other NVIDIA-entry threads have zero first-window hits. The mapped compiler file
  identifies NVIDIA GPU Compiler Driver 610.88, file version 32.0.16.1088. All function names in
  the two dominant thread reports remain unresolved; module/offset evidence does not name the
  internal work or explain the render thread's kernel cost.
  The capture narrows the first-use cost to sampled native execution, including NVIDIA's compiler
  module, rather than merely a worker's entry-address provenance. It establishes no production
  optimization, stable speedup or new full-suite runtime. Independent capture/result review is
  clean. The temporary source is removed after verifying its archived hash. Restored Release
  compilation passes in 39.330s with actual test Csc confirmed and temporary markers absent.
  This capture's promised fresh pre-probe binary/cache/shader snapshots were not retained. The corrected
  restoration comparison instead uses the preceding thread-CPU probe's independently verified
  archives: all five current DLLs match those bytes. This is explicit inherited-baseline evidence,
  not a newly captured before/after pair. The failed missing-snapshot proof is preserved and
  superseded; its empty comparison cannot establish identity. Discovery returns to the original
  5,950 identities with zero differences. Nine cold mechanics cases and both cached-patch controls
  pass 11/11 in 13.904s with no failures/skips. The 909 Release shader modules and manifest match
  the same trusted prior receipt. Its 434 valid cache payloads (17,523,863 bytes) also retain their
  hashes/modification times; one read-recency record advances separately. These comparisons share
  the inherited-baseline limitation and do not replace a missing fresh pre-capture snapshot.
  Independent capture, result, prose and restoration review is clean. Only this evidence document
  remains modified; no new full-suite or Debug qualification is established.
  Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/FirstDrawNativeCpuStacks/20261105T110246Z/.

First-use driver diagnostics and context control (2026-11-05; measured and removed)
  The user requested identification of the delay after a successful native program-binary load.
  Source inspection confirms the accepted cache path skips native stage creation/specialization;
  LastLoadWasHit is published only after that binary candidate completes successfully. This is
  not an application-requested fallback to GLSL or SPIR-V compilation. OpenGL nevertheless permits
  state-dependent recompilation after restoring a binary and only requires sufficient retained
  information to support it. Deferred post-draw export may reduce such work but is not guaranteed
  to eliminate it; the earlier counterbalanced export probe already found repeated multi-second
  first draws for new owners in the same process, including post-draw binaries and identical scenes.
  Reference: [ARB_get_program_binary](https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL/extensions/ARB/ARB_get_program_binary.txt),
  program-binary semantics on external GL state and deferred retrieval.
  One existing regular cached-patch Debug case passes in 12.561s process wall, with no GLDBG lines.
  Its best-effort callback setup and console delivery were not independently verified, so this
  silence supplies no compiler-message evidence. A temporary isolated Release diagnostic instead
  installs a synchronous all-message callback before program creation and asserts delivery of a
  known application marker. It captures up to 4,096 records in memory, with phase/timestamps and
  no callback-thread output, and emits records after consecutive first/repeat draws. The exact
  scene, four independent oracles and all 28 seven-MRT float bit comparisons remain unchanged.
  The diagnostic passes 1/1 on an actual cache hit, with sentinel observed, zero overflow and zero
  callback exceptions. First helper wall is 2368.401ms; repeat is 2.375ms. Its 17 messages comprise
  one marker, two first-draw buffer-placement notifications and 14 post-draw readback notifications.
  There are no shader-compiler/performance/recompilation messages. Verified silence cannot rule
  out internal compilation, already implicated by sampled instruction addresses in the prior trace.
  A second temporary control varies only the headless context's requested debug flag, verifies the
  actual flag and uses a receipt-local cache whose keys include context flags. Two cache-miss seed
  skips populate separate entries; neither is counted as a rendering pass. Both measured reloads
  pass with cache hits, verified callback delivery and unchanged original oracles/bit comparisons.
  Debug context flags 3: first 2016.834ms, repeat 1.676ms. Ordinary context flags 1: first 1711.247ms,
  repeat 1.468ms. Removing the debug flag does not eliminate the multi-second first-use cost. One
  instrumented timing pair does not establish a stable percentage or attribute the difference to
  that flag. Synchronous callback collection can perturb timings; no full-suite result follows.
  The supported boundary is native first-use work associated with a newly restored executable;
  the exact compiler operation, state dependency and render-thread kernel cause remain unresolved.
  Neither public symbols nor verified driver diagnostics identify those internals. No production
  cache, shader or context policy is changed. Callback/user-data/enable flags are restored; the
  unqueryable prior message-filter matrix is explicitly not restored within the exiting isolated
  process. The temporary test is removed and the fixture restored byte-for-byte to its archived
  original. Restored Release compilation passes in 14.263s with actual test Csc confirmed. All five
  DLLs match this investigation's fresh pre-probe snapshots, and temporary class/environment markers
  are absent. Discovery returns to the original 5,950 identities with zero differences; nine cold
  mechanics cases and both cached-patch controls pass 11/11 in 9.888s with no failures/skips.
  Both configurations retain all 909 shader modules and their manifests. Debug's 78 default-cache
  payloads and Release's 434 retain hashes/modification times, with no new default keys. Restored
  controls advance one Release read-recency record separately; isolated context entries remain
  receipt-local. Only this evidence document remains modified.
  Independent source, raw measurement, documentation and restoration reviews are clean.
  Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/FirstDrawDriverDiagnostics/20261105T115333Z/.

Direct native binary first-use reproduction (2026-11-05; measured)
  The user approved a standalone direct-OpenGL reproduction and isolated state comparisons.
  The retained console project under the receipt uses OpenTK 4.9.4 and native GLFW only, with
  no VGE/game assembly or project references, rendering wrappers, interception, or fallback shader
  compilation. Offline restore/build uses cached packages; no original project is rebuilt.
  A read-only copy of the existing diagnostic cache entry is verified against its index before
  use: native format 36385, 413,252 bytes, SHA-256 0E6F51BF122D79B406BEA80ADED6BA227F65A39E3DF845C38816BC33BDB657F7.
  Native ProgramBinary must link successfully. The live context reports RTX 4090, NVIDIA 610.88,
  OpenGL 4.5, core-profile mask 1 and context flags 3. Direct calls recreate the regular cached-patch
  textures, UBO layouts, triangle geometry and seven RGBA32F one-pixel attachments. Sampler and
  block assignments are validated numerically when names are unavailable. Packed UBO hashes are
  retained. System.Numerics inverse-matrix arithmetic is not claimed byte-identical to Mat4f;
  baseline equivalence is gated by the original four assertions and 28 first/repeat float bit
  comparisons, not an unmeasured canonical-buffer byte comparison.
  Baseline passes binary admission, all original assertions, all 28 comparisons and GL error
  checks. Contiguous first-use wall is 2496.563ms; the separately summed timed stages are
  2496.504ms. Native DrawElements returns in .079ms, its following GetError takes 2468.213ms,
  and Finish takes .569ms. Repeat draw/error/Finish take .001/.0196/.0443ms. Both completion
  and error-query costs remain explicit; this protocol includes Finish/error checks between draws
  and is not the earlier unsynchronized first/repeat timing protocol.
  With only draw-buffer zero active, all seven attachments/data/formats remain allocated and
  unchanged. That run passes the two decision-output assertions and four first/repeat comparisons;
  the other six outputs are intentionally unverified. First-use wall remains 2422.792ms, with
  DrawElements .0776ms, GetError 2393.080ms and Finish .3545ms. Both runs use identical copied
  binary/context/UBO hashes. Reducing active output count does not eliminate the delay; one
  pair and uncontrolled driver-cache warmth establish no stable percentage or MRT cause.
  An additional native loading-policy comparison requests the binary-retrieval hint on/off and
  verifies its effective value after loading. The enabled run passes all baseline assertions and
  28 comparisons, with first-use wall 1996.003ms and first GetError 1968.395ms. The disabled request
  reports effective hint 1 after successful binary loading and therefore fails the factor gate
  before drawing. There is no qualifying disabled-hint timing and no causal hint comparison.
  An earlier incorrect pre-load equality assertion also stopped before binary loading; it supplies
  no performance evidence. It was corrected because the hint takes effect at successful link/load,
  as described in [ARB_get_program_binary](https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL/extensions/ARB/ARB_get_program_binary.txt)
  and OpenGL 4.6 sections 7.3/7.5. The observed post-load value is not presented as a conformance bug.
  Initial compile/API and texture-binding setup defects were corrected and independently reviewed
  before any qualifying execution; their failed/superseded receipts remain separate.
  The significant first-use stall reproduces with native calls and the copied executable outside
  VGE's rendering path. Together with the earlier CPU samples, this points to native driver work;
  these wall clocks alone do not identify a compiler pass, exact state dependency or kernel cause.
  No production/test behavior is changed and no full-suite speedup is established. All five
  original DLLs and ten captured source/project files match fresh before/after hashes. Both sets
  of 909 shader payloads and manifests remain unchanged. Release's 434 cache payloads (17,523,863
  bytes) and Debug's 78 (5,751,747 bytes), plus their index/metadata files, retain hashes, sizes
  and modification times; even default-store recency is unchanged. No canonical build/test was
  repeated because none of its sources or binaries changed. Independent source, measurement,
  documentation and invariant reviews are clean. The standalone source, inputs, executable
  snapshots and raw commands/results are retained.
  Receipts and runnable reproduction: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/FirstDrawDirectOpenGl/20261105T123416Z/.

SPIR-V miss versus native-binary hit benchmark (2026-11-05; measured and removed)
  The user requested the cost of the actual production loading paths, including compilation
  versus restoring a previously compiled native binary. After the user requested benchmark
  tooling, the measurement uses BenchmarkDotNet 0.15.8 rather than ordinary xUnit test durations.
  The superseded xUnit timing harness was compiled but never measured. Framework-validation and
  packaging attempts that executed zero workloads are retained separately and supply no samples.
  ColdStart jobs use four fresh process launches per path, zero warmups, one iteration, one
  invocation and unroll factor one. No diagnosers or automatic power-plan changes are enabled.
  A workload guard rejects repeated invocations; managed-thread/current-context guards retain
  OpenGL affinity. Setup creates the fixture and isolated store without creating the program.
  The benchmark measures Programs.Create through scene setup, first RenderQuadTo and WaitIdle.
  Repeat rendering, readback, four original assertions and 28 first/repeat float bit comparisons
  run afterward, outside the benchmark interval. There is no cross-mode 28-float bit assertion.
  Both paths use an enabled ProgramBinaryStore and the same regular cached-patch water scene,
  selected shader assets, typed inputs and seven-MRT outputs. Binary cases start with a copy of
  the validated existing native cache entry and require a real hit. Each SPIR-V case starts with
  its own empty store and requires a miss; normal native-binary export/save is INCLUDED in its
  create/load cost. This compares actual hit/miss behavior, not isolated compiler-only time.
  The runner references the prebuilt canonical test/production assemblies without rebuilding
  the shader toolchain under BenchmarkDotNet's redirected output. Matching library-only xUnit
  dependencies avoid test-runner entry points. All 1,489 manifest-listed assets are explicitly
  staged and hashed. Generated outputs use a short owned workspace path to fit Windows limits;
  completed outputs are archived under the receipt. No global path, registry or cache setting changes.
  Eight launches qualify: four hits and four misses, each with exactly one actual operation,
  successful post-timing assertions and exit zero. All actual samples remain in WorkloadResult;
  detected outliers were not removed. Primary BenchmarkDotNet first-use seconds are:
    binary: 2.5328527, 2.6640870, 3.2448363, 2.5341002;
    SPIR-V: 7.2215005, 6.0866240, 5.8649709, 5.8033370.
  Binary mean/median are 2.744/2.599s, range 2.533-3.245s. SPIR-V mean/median are 6.244/5.976s,
  range 5.803-7.222s. Median internal phases are binary load .237s/setup .053s/first helper 2.315s,
  versus SPIR-V load 5.798s/setup .054s/first helper .120s. Completion waits are below .5ms
  in both paths. Diagnostic repeats are approximately 2-4ms. Phase medians do not sum
  exactly to the primary benchmark median; the primary interval also includes its entry guards.
  In these samples, native caching reduces mean first-use cost by about 3.500s, despite moving
  substantial work into the first draw. This supports a benefit for this shader/configuration;
  it does not identify the internal NVIDIA operation or establish a whole-suite speedup.
  Actual execution groups four binary launches before four SPIR-V launches, not the earlier
  planned ABBA/BAAB ordering. Driver and OS caches remain uncontrolled and retained. ColdStart
  means fresh benchmark processes here, not a purged driver cache; the descending SPIR-V samples
  are preserved rather than attributed to a specific warming mechanism. Small grouped samples
  do not establish a stable universal percentage or apply to every shader. Power-plan receipts match.
  The temporary workload source is removed after verifying its measured hash. Restored Release
  compilation passes in 16.239s with actual test Csc confirmed. All five DLLs and ten source/helper
  files match fresh before snapshots. Discovery returns to the same 5,950 identities with zero
  differences; nine cold mechanics cases and both cached-patch controls pass 11/11 in 10.127s,
  without failures/skips. Both configurations retain all 909 shader payloads and manifests.
  Release's 434 default-cache payloads (17,523,863 bytes) and Debug's 78 (5,751,747 bytes) retain
  hashes/modification times, with no key or format/size/digest changes. Only one Release read-recency
  value advances during restored controls; Debug's index is unchanged. The owned short-output
  directory is archived and absent; no owned process remains. Independent source, measurement,
  documentation and restoration reviews are clean.
  Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/SpirvVersusNativeBinary/20261105T135204Z/.
